# Autonomous research

Karpathy's *autoresearch* is a deceptively simple idea: give an agent a real (small) ML
training setup and let it experiment overnight. It edits the training code, runs a
fixed-budget experiment, checks whether the metric improved, keeps the change or reverts
it, and repeats. You wake up to a log of experiments and, hopefully, a better model. The
key move is that **you never touch the Python** — you write the *agent's instructions*
and let it drive the code. You're programming the research org, not the model.

That's a precise fit for Workbooks: the experimenter is an [`agent`](/tooling/authoring-an-agent)
(prose brain + `bash` over a `/work` sandbox), and "keep what improves, loop" is a tiny
`server` around it. This lesson builds the whole loop.

## The experimenter

The brain describes how to run *one* honest experiment — and nothing else. Its body is
its system prompt:

```
agent :researcher do
  You run ONE machine-learning experiment in /work. `train.py` is yours to edit — the
  model, optimizer, hyperparameters, anything. `prepare.py` is fixed; do not change it.
  The metric is val_bpb from a fixed-budget run — LOWER is better.

  Procedure:
    1. Form ONE clear hypothesis ("a wider MLP will lower val_bpb").
    2. Make the smallest edit to train.py that tests it — change one thing only.
    3. Run it with bash and read the real output. Do not estimate the result.
    4. On the LAST line, report exactly: val_bpb=<number>

  Change one variable at a time so every delta is attributable. Be honest — report the
  number you measured, never the number you hoped for.
end
```

Notice what's *not* here: no training framework, no experiment tracker, no harness. The
instructions are the experiment design — which is exactly the part a researcher should
be iterating on.

## The loop

Around the experimenter sits the org: run N experiments, and **keep an edit only when
the metric actually improves** — otherwise discard it and carry the previous best code
forward. `Nexus.Agent.run/1` returns the agent's final `answer` (where it reported the
score) and `vfs_files` (the code it left behind), which is all the loop needs:

```
server :lab do
  @researcher "…the :researcher instructions above, as a string…"

  # Run `rounds` experiments; the best code + a log fall out the bottom.
  def run(files, rounds \\ 50) do
    init = %{files: files, best: 99.9, kept: 0, log: []}

    Enum.reduce(1..rounds, init, fn n, st ->
      {:ok, %{answer: a, vfs_files: edited}} =
        Nexus.Agent.run(
          system: @researcher,
          task: "Experiment ##{n}. Current best val_bpb is #{st.best} — try to beat it.",
          seed: st.files,
          timeout_ms: 600_000
        )

      case parse_bpb(a) do
        score when score < st.best ->
          %{st | files: edited, best: score, kept: st.kept + 1,
                 log: [%{n: n, kept: true, score: score} | st.log]}

        score ->
          # no improvement → revert: keep the previous best `files`, drop `edited`
          %{st | log: [%{n: n, kept: false, score: score} | st.log]}
      end
    end)
  end

  defp parse_bpb(text) do
    case Regex.run(~r/val_bpb=([\d.]+)/, text || "") do
      [_, v] -> String.to_float(v)
      _ -> 99.9              # a run that didn't report a score never wins
    end
  end
end
```

That's the entire research org. `seed: st.files` hands the current best code to each
experiment; on success the loop adopts `edited`, on failure it simply doesn't — the
revert is "don't update the accumulator." After `rounds` runs you have the best `files`,
the best score, and a `log` of what was tried and what stuck.

## Tuning the org, not the code

This is the payoff of programming the agent instead of the Python: to change the research
*strategy*, you edit prose, not a framework. Want bolder hypotheses? Loosen the
"smallest edit" rule. Want it to chase a specific axis? Say so in `:researcher`. Want a
breadth-first sweep instead of a hill-climb? Run many experimenters from different seeds
at once — the same fan-out as [agents at scale](/examples/agents-at-scale) — and keep the
best across all of them.

## What to take away

- **You program the agent, not the code.** The instructions *are* the experiment design —
  the same role as autoresearch's `program.md`.
- **"Keep what improves" is the whole engine.** A `reduce` that adopts `vfs_files` on a
  better score and reverts otherwise.
- **The sandbox makes it safe to let it run unattended** — real edits, real runs, all
  contained in `/work`. → [Sandboxing & the Dock](/capabilities/sandboxing-and-the-dock)
